the 2019 general election results look  pretty definitive for the Conservatives  the Tories got the most votes won the  most seats got the biggest majority in a  generation but that doesn't tell the  full story smaller parties saw a massive  increase in supports but got very little  forests the Greens vote share when top  60 percents  the largest of any party but they still  only return one MP despite getting 3.7  million votes the Liberal Democrats only  returns and Levin seats in contrast the  SNP got 48 seats but only had 1.2  million votes less than half the Lib  Dems elsewhere worries about splitting  the remain votes that some people to  vote tactically but it didn't seem to  help in fact while 52 percent of the  total votes went to party supporting a  second referendum the pro brexit Tory  still got their huge majority these  issues have led people from all sides of  the political spectrum to call for  electoral reform so let's take a look at  some of the options what do we have at  the moment our current system is called  first-past-the-post and it's pretty  simple basically the candidates who win  the most votes get to become MPs and the  party with an overall majority of MPs  gets to form a government this  simplicity is why it's favoured by many  but others say it can be unfair causing  millions of votes to be meaningless and  leading to things like tactical voting  what are the alternatives reformers  often call for proportional  representation basically this means that  MPs would be elected based on the  percentage of votes they receive so if a  party wins 20 percent of the overall  votes they would get 20 percent of the  seats in parliament there are loads of  different types of PR one of the most  popular systems is party-list  proportional representation it's used in  80 countries around the world including  in the UK when we elect MEP s under  party list the public votes for the  party not as specific candidates  the party's draw up a list of candidates  and rank them in a preferable order the  number of candidates who get elected  from that list depends on the percentage  of votes they receive some critics say  PR is too complicated with return to  many coalition governments others points  the fact that a more proportional system  could give extremist parties an easier  route into UK politics for example in  the 2005 general election the British  National Party got as high as 17 percent  of the vote in some areas under a  proportional representation system  parties like that would have a better  chance of getting in another popular  option for change is alternative vote AV  is technically not a form of  proportional representation but some  argue it's fairer than  first-past-the-post under a V voters  ranks their favored candidates on the  ballot paper if more than half the  voters put the same candidate as their  favor they become the MP if not the  counters take a look at the second-place  votes and so on and so on until one  candidate gets a majority we actually  had a referendum of our AV back in 2011  but it was rejected by the British  public will we ever actually see  electoral reform in short probably not  realistic we only parties with the power  to change the systems and make them more  proportional of the Tories and labour  and those parties happen to benefit the  most from first-past-the-post so why  would they vote for something  differently and after the huge majority  first-past-the-post returned for Boris  Johnson it may be a while until we see  any meaningful change
 

